Diabetic Foot Ulcer
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Painless plantar ulceration, Surrounding thick callus, Loss of protective sensation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-015
Reason for Visit: Sudden discovery of a deep, painless wound on the sole of the foot with malodorous discharge.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a diabetic patient whose daughter noticed a deep, foul-smelling ulcer on the patient's foot that the patient was completely unaware of. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for deep tissue infection, osteomyelitis, and systemic sepsis, and to initiate immediate limb-salvaging wound care and antibiotic therapy before the next routine in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I didn't even know it was there. My daughter saw blood on my sock and checked my foot. It doesn't hurt at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a 15-year history of poorly controlled Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with known profound distal symmetrical sensorimotor polyneuropathy. Today, the daughter removed the patient's sock and discovered a deep painless ulceration on the plantar surface under the first metatarsal head. The ulcer is actively weeping a seropurulent, malodorous fluid. The patient reports no pain, fever, or chills. The foot has a history of foot deformities including mild Charcot arthropathy. The patient exhibits a complete loss of protective sensation, unaware of the trauma that caused the wound.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, high risk of ongoing silent trauma.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe neuropathy preventing the patient from sensing tissue damage; high risk of limb amputation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 95 Kg
General: Well-appearing, comfortable, completely unbothered by the severe foot wound.
Physical Exam: The right foot is warm and dry secondary to autonomic neuropathy. Bounding pedal pulses are palpable. On the plantar surface beneath the first metatarsal head, there is a 2x2 cm full-thickness ulceration. It is surrounded by a thick rim of hyperkeratotic callus. The ulcer base contains slough and is actively exuding a mild, malodorous seropurulent discharge. Careful probing with a sterile instrument does not definitively contact bone, but the wound is deep. There is localized erythema extending 1 cm from the wound edge, but no ascending cellulitis or fluctuance. Monofilament testing confirms total loss of protective sensation to a 10g monofilament bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: Patient walking barefoot in the house prior to examination.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient on Metformin and Insulin, adherence is reportedly poor.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Infected Neuropathic Diabetic Foot Ulcer - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Arterial Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of bounding pedal pulses, warm skin, and the completely painless nature of the wound, which starkly contrasts with painful, ischemic arterial ulcers.

Venous Stasis Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Location on the plantar weight-bearing surface rather than the medial malleolus, and the absence of significant dependent edema or hemosiderin staining.

Squamous Cell Carcinoma (Marjolin's Ulcer) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic location under a pressure point with thick callus in a severely neuropathic diabetic foot clearly points to mechanical trauma and neuropathy as the primary etiology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic, deeply infected neuropathic diabetic foot ulcer characterized by complete painlessness, thick surrounding callus, and a plantar weight-bearing location. Triage correctly identified the sudden discovery of a malodorous, bleeding wound in a neuropathic patient as a high-risk scenario for silent osteomyelitis and impending sepsis. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was critical to probe the wound depth, evaluate the extent of the local infection, rule out systemic toxicity, and immediately initiate broad-spectrum oral antibiotics and strict offloading instructions. Failing to intervene promptly would likely result in rapid deep tissue destruction and highly probable limb loss.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansed the wound with saline. Debrided loose, non-viable superficial slough. Applied a sterile, absorbent antimicrobial (silver-based) dressing. Commenced oral Amoxicillin-Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for localized infection.
Positive Behavioral Change: By wearing supportive, enclosed shoes at all times—even indoors—you will mechanically protect your numb foot from further invisible injuries, preventing this ulcer from worsening or new ones from forming. By keeping your foot completely elevated and avoiding standing on it (strict offloading), you will remove the pressure that caused the hole, giving the tissue a chance to finally close and heal.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral generated to the High-Risk Foot Clinic / Podiatry.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended immediate use of a post-op stiff-soled shoe or walker boot if available.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 24-48 hours for wound reassessment and coordination of High-Risk Foot Clinic care.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness rapidly spreads up the foot or leg, if the foot becomes entirely black, or if the patient develops a fever, chills, or sudden confusion, present immediately to the Emergency Department via ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diabetic foot wounds are limb-threatening emergencies. The true depth and involvement of the bone requires an X-ray and specialist review.
Clinical Course: Healing will be extremely prolonged and requires strict adherence to offloading (not walking on it) and diabetic control.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The daughter must inspect the foot twice daily. Any spreading redness or onset of fever requires an immediate emergency response.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your diabetes has destroyed the nerves in your foot, so you cannot feel when the skin is crushed and torn. The wound is infected. The antibiotics will treat the surface infection, but taking all pressure off the foot is the only way it will heal.
Actionable Steps: Do absolutely no barefoot walking. Keep the dressing dry and intact. Take the antibiotics continuously.

